{"study_accession":"PRJNA1243302","secondary_study_accession":"SRP573860","title":"Impacts of Inorganic Arsenic Exposure on Genetic Stability of Human Mesenchymal Stromal Cells (hMSCs)","description":"Human mesenchymal stem/stromal cells (hMSCs) can differentiate into mesoderm-type cells, making them suitable candidates for tissue repair therapies. However, their relatively low frequency in adult tissue necessitates ex vivo expansion prior to regenerative medicine applications. Expansion can lead to selection of chromosomally abnormal clones and therefore, long-term hMSC stability should be studied. hMSCs applications in regenerative medicine ensures commercial availability of normal karyotype human primary cells for toxicity assessment and hMSCs could serve as alternatives to immortalized human cell models. In this work, we evaluated the potential of hMSCs in toxicity testing using inorganic arsenic (iAs) as a case study. hMSCs were exposed to iAs at different durations to track cellular aging and study long-term genetic stability. iAs exposures (48 hours) resulted in micronuclei induction. hMSCs were also exposed to iAs for 6 days to determine if hMSCs would become more susceptible to chromosomal damage following exposure to the model genotoxicant, mitomycin C (MMC). The culture duration and iAs exposure did not alter MMC potency, indicating that the hMSCs susceptibility to chromosomal damage remained unchanged. We also used gene expression analysis to investigate the molecular impacts of iAs on hMSCs over the course of short (3 days total) and long (30 days total) experiments. Both iAs exposures activated biomarkers associated with oxidative stress, but not biomarkers for direct DNA damage, providing support for an indirect mode of action for iAs genotoxicity. Overall, this study establishes the utility of hMSCs as a new model for toxicity screening and provides mechanistic information underlying iAs toxicity. Overall design: Gene expression profiles were compared for hMSCs treated for 3 and 30 days with doses of sodium arsenite versus a solvent control (water).","pubmed":"40241300","geo_series":"","n_samples":36,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN47618516","study_accession":"PRJNA1243302","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"hMSCs, 3uM 3 days culture, Sample36","description":"hMSCs, 3uM 3 days culture, Sample36","sex":"male","geo_sample":"GSM8877899","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-07-27","attributes":[["source_name","bone marrow"],["tissue","bone marrow"],["cell_type","mesenchymal stromal cells"],["genotype","WT"],["sex","male"],["chemical","iAsIII"],["chemical longname","sodium arsenite"],["treatment","3µM iAs"],["time","3 days"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR32899462","experiment_accession":"SRX28174789","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":23598633,"base_count":1758762306,"first_public":"2025-07-27","experiment_title":"NextSeq 500 sequencing: GSM8877899: hMSCs, 3uM 3 days culture, Sample36 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN47618517","study_accession":"PRJNA1243302","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"hMSCs, 3uM 3 days culture, Sample35","description":"hMSCs, 3uM 3 days culture, Sample35","sex":"male","geo_sample":"GSM8877898","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-07-27","attributes":[["source_name","bone marrow"],["tissue","bone marrow"],["cell_type","mesenchymal stromal cells"],["genotype","WT"],["sex","male"],["chemical","iAsIII"],["chemical longname","sodium arsenite"],["treatment","3µM iAs"],["time","3 days"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR32899463","experiment_accession":"SRX28174788","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":22473373,"base_count"